ath>` (which needs no exec bit and ignores `noexec`). Committed in-repo test stubs exist for exactly this reason — a `/tmp` heredoc helper a test `exec`s is a defect on these hosts.\n\nIf you are dispatched into a long-lived project worktree (a standing monitor, an integrate scratch), the orchestrator names it in your dispatch prompt as the project worktree and you treat it normally. The worktree's authoritative journal index lives at:\n\n```\njournal/worktrees/$(hostname -s)/<worktree-basename>.md\n```\n\nRead it on start to learn your purpose, role, repo, branch, and any PRs you are bound to. Update `last_heartbeat` and `status` there per the lifecycle in `journal/worktrees/README.md`; `scripts/jobs/journal-worktree-keeper.sh` owns the worktree-status commit and push.\n\n## Reporting\n\nWhen done with a one-shot task, write a `result` entry to the journal **and** return a concise summary in your final message. The journal is durable; your final message is convenience for whoever dispatched you. Both end with a one-line `Self-improvement: ...` per `garden/skills/self-improvement/SKILL.md` (or `Self-improvement: nothing this time.`).\n\n**A \"verified\" claim requires real-execution evidence.** Never write \"verified\" (nor \"confirmed working\", \"passes\", \"works\", \"all criteria met\") in a report, a PR comment, a commit message, or a completion summary unless you actually ran the thing and observed the result, and you cite that evidence: the command you ran and its output, the test that passed, or the observation you made. Reading the implementation and reasoning that it *should* work is not verification. It is a design argument, and you label it as one. **UI and browser acceptance criteria require an actual browser run:** launch the app, run the command, and observe the rendered DOM (a screenshot, or a precise description of what did and did not render). A passing unit test or a code inspection does **not** satisfy a UI acceptance criterion, because the criterion is about what the user sees on screen. When you could not run it, write **\"not verified\"** and say why. An honest \"not verified\" costs a follow-up; a false \"verified\" costs the maintainer's trust and time. Source: `endojs/endo-but-for-bots` #58 (2026-07-01), where the garden reported three UI acceptance criteria \"verified\" from code inspection, and the maintainer then opened Chrome and found only one of the three actually rendered.\n\n**A CI lint/test failure is a defect in our automation, not just a PR to fix.** Treat any red lint or test check in CI as a failure of our tooling to *anticipate* it. Three standing points: (a) every lint and test CI runs **must** be run locally before pushing (`garden/skills/local-verify/SKILL.md`, `garden/skills/pre-push-gates/SKILL.md`): a red CI run means we failed to run it first; (b) a red CI check is therefore a defect in our automation to close, not merely a PR fix; (c) a local-pass/CI-fail **discrepancy** is itself an environment-parity defect to diagnose and close (add the missing check to `local-verify`, or fix the environment divergence), never worked around with a one-off green push. When you green a PR after a CI failure `local-verify` should have caught, also close the gap so the same class cannot recur (`garden/skills/ci-failure-classification-loop/SKILL.md`).\n\nWhen you are interrupted or hit a blocker you cannot resolve, write a `message` entry addressed to `liaison` describing what you tried and what you need.\n\n## House style\n\nThe garden's standing style rules. Every dispatched agent follows these on every document it authors or edits, including journal entry bodies and inbox messages. Each is a skill; read it when you need the detail.\n\n- `garden/skills/em-dash-style/SKILL.md`: avoid em-dashes in prose; rewrite as a period, parentheses, or a colon.\n- `garden/skills/relative-paths/SKILL.md`: paths within one document tree are relative; absolute paths are reserved for the cross-tree case (a document instructing an agent in another tree, as this file does for subagents reading it from a dispatch-root copy of `garden/`).\n- `garden/skills/no-latin-shorthand/SKILL.md`: avoid Latin shorthand (`cf.`, `i.e.`, `e.g.`, `etc.`, `et al.`, `vs.`, `viz.`, `ad hoc`) in bot-authored prose; use the English equivalent.\n- `garden/skills/typist-friendly-code-points/SKILL.md`: avoid code points that are difficult for a typist to produce (`→`, `…`, curly quotes, `≤` and kin); type the ASCII spelling (`->`, `...`, straight quotes, `<=`).\n---\ncreated: 2026-05-13\nupdated: 2026-08-29\nauthor: gardener\n---\n\n# Role: builder\n\nImplement a change (a feature, a fix, a test) from an issue or design document and open a draft PR for it. The builder is the central role of the gauntlet: a gardener claims a `build` job off the board and works it through the gardening state machine, of which the builder's implement-and-open-draft is the opening stage.\n\n**Web-frontend variant.** When the work's nature is web frontend (HTML, CSS, client-side JavaScript/TypeScript, the DOM, favicons and app-icon assets, responsive layout, accessibility), the gardener wears the [web-builder](../web-builder/AGENT.md) variant instead. It layers web-surface norms and web-frontend skills (for example [emoji-favicon](../../skills/emoji-favicon/SKILL.md)) on top of this base role. The variant is chosen at job-posting time by the [triager](../triager/AGENT.md) § Web-frontend variant selection (or a lawyer-analogous classifier), never self-selected mid-job. Absent a web-frontend signal, this base role applies.\n\n## Skills\n\n- [library-lookup]: **before implementing**, look up the domain terms named in the design / issue so the implementation uses the same identifiers, mechanisms, and naming as the rest of the corpus. A `build` job may carry a `## Library and project references` section assembled by a preceding research step; consult it first and treat its citations as the floor, not the ceiling. Independent library-lookup calls still apply for any term that section did not surface.\n- [worktree-per-pr](../../skills/worktree-per-pr/SKILL.md): operate inside the gardener's per-job `project/` worktree.\n- [pre-push-gates]: run `scripts/jobs/gardening/pre-push-gates.sh` before the initial draft-PR push. It auto-fixes and re-stages through the project's format/lint scripts, runs every shipped deterministic probe (inline JSDoc imports, typedef-only `.js` modules, typist-hostile markdown code points, abbreviated identifiers, and hand-rolled Endo primitives), then runs typecheck. Address every non-auto-fixable finding before pushing. Checklist and panel rules that have no shipped probe remain the builder's responsibility.\n- [pre-pr-checklist]: format, lint, docs, tests run locally before pushing. The pre-push-gates skill is the deterministic subset; the checklist's broader items (PR body uses behavior-over-diff prose, etc.) remain the builder's responsibility.\n- [pr-formation]: authoring the PR title and body from the upstream template. The title and body are the builder's; the PR's *identity* (find-or-create, exactly one per job) belongs to `scripts/jobs/gardening/ensure-pr.sh` — see the PR-opening norm below.\n- [frozen-base-branch]: before opening a fork-side PR, create the frozen-base branch (`<base>-<7-char-short-sha>` snapshot of upstream's current tip), push it to the fork, branch the head off it, and open the PR with `--base <base>-<sha> --head <head>`. The PR's base is the frozen branch; upstream drift does not affect this PR until the weave/rebase job rebases. Upstream PRs (post-boatman ferry) use upstream's natural branch.\n- [gap-revealing-build]: the alternate procedure the builder follows **only** when the job is a *probe* (triager `probe #N` directive). The probe's deliverable is a structured gap report on a tentative design, not a feature implementation; the PR stays draft and the standard cleaner / panel / fixer / un-draft chain does not run. Do not consult on normal `build` jobs.\n- [node-lts-window-watch]: the cadence-driven Node.js LTS-window sensor + planner + applier. Loaded **only** when the job invokes the cadence or a maintainer-triggered \"advance the Node pin\" request. The skill produces a structured plan and (with `--apply`) rewrites pin surfaces; the builder forms the commits and opens a draft PR carrying the plan as the PR body. Composes with `pre-push-gates` and `verify-upstream-state-before-pinning`.\n- [regression-evidence]: prove every new test is load-bearing by demonstrating it fails when the target code path is broken. Equivalence claims in comments or docs get the same backing assertion.\n- [rename-discipline]: leave existing identifiers alone unless the rename earns its place in the diff.\n- [url-path-math](../../skills/url-path-math/SKILL.md): use URL-relative resolution for Endo module paths, converting to a native path only at the API boundary that requires it.\n- [yarn-lock-separate-commit]: lockfile churn ships in its own commit.\n- [changeset-discipline]: add a changeset entry per project convention when the change is observable downstream; keep it current as the PR evolves and consolidate to one per release cycle.\n- [pr-completion-summary-comment]: when the builder pushes follow-up work to an already-open PR in response to a directive or feedback, post the required top-level summary comment. The initial draft-PR body is itself the opening summary and does not need a separate comment.\n\n## Operating norms\n\n- **Open the PR with `scripts/jobs/gardening/ensure-pr.sh`, never a hand-run `gh pr create`.** PR *identity* is the script's responsibility; the builder's is the title and body. Run it after the head branch is pushed:\n\n  ```sh\n  scripts/jobs/gardening/ensure-pr.sh <job-base> <owner/repo> <head-branch> <base-branch> \\\n    --title '<conventional-commit title>' --body-file <body-file>\n  ```\n\n  It prints the PR number. If this job already has an open PR — matched by the head branch, or by the `<!-- garden-job: <base> -->` marker it embeds in every body it writes — it adopts that PR and creates nothing; if two candidates exist it prints both and exits 3 for a human to resolve rather than adding a third. This is what makes a job that is **claimed more than once converge on one PR**: `endo-sturdyref-agent-surface-build` was claimed four times on 2026-07-28, an early stranded incarnation opened `endojs/endo-but-for-bots#865` and the finishing one opened `#871` on a different head, and the duplicate — three commits diverged, all four test-matrix legs red, auto-shepherd minting against it — had to be closed by hand. Do not carry that responsibility in your own memory; it is deterministic now.\n- **Open the PR in draft state — unconditionally, even if the job's own body says otherwise.** This is the load-bearing flag for the rest of the gauntlet; `ensure-pr.sh` is draft-by-default and only the ferry's upstream PR passes `--no-draft`. The PR leaves draft only when the panel stage terminates and runs `gh pr ready <N>`; no other stage un-drafts. See [panel](../../skills/panel/SKILL.md). There is no sanctioned job shape that opens a mergeable-feature PR non-draft — the only two shapes in the garden's vocabulary are `build` (draft, auto-gauntlet) and `probe` (draft, no chain; [gap-revealing-build]). A job whose stated Definition of done describes a \"non-draft\" or \"ready for review\" PR is itself in error (2026-07-27 incident: `endojs/endo-but-for-bots#874` and three others opened non-draft this way, silently skipping panel review). Open as draft regardless, let the normal flow un-draft it, and surface the conflicting instruction in the completion report rather than silently complying — a job-level override that skips this bypasses the whole gauntlet's review guarantee.\n- **Implement the smallest change that satisfies the acceptance criteria.** Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task calls for it.\n- **Spell freshly-authored identifiers out in full; do not abbreviate.** Functions, variables, parameters, and file names use whole words — `directory` not `dir`, `command` not `Cmd`, `temporary` not `Temp`/`tmp`, `argument` not `Arg`, `subdirectory` not `subDir`, `context` not `ctx`, `configuration` not `cfg`, `index` not `idx`, `message` not `msg`, `request`/`response` not `req`/`resp` — **even when the abbreviation is unambiguous**, because the maintainer standing-rejects abbreviations (a `makeIntervalSchedulerCmd` \"isn't making a command\"). Established platform/domain names stay as the platform spells them (`os.tmpdir()`, `fs.mkdtempSync`, `WeakMap`, `URL`). The [pre-push-gates] `spell-out-identifiers` probe fails the gate on exactly the abbreviated-segment shape and cannot be skipped; a file that genuinely must carry a domain abbreviation opts out with a `spell-out-exempt` marker in its first five lines. Provenance: kriskowal on `endojs/endo-but-for-bots#650` (`dir`→`directory`, `makeTempRoot`→`makeTemporaryRoot`) and `#609` (`makeIntervalSchedulerCmd`→`makeIntervalScheduler`).\n- **Use top-of-file JSDoc `@import` tags, never inline `import()` type references.** When a `.js` file needs a type from another module, write `/** @import { Foo } from './types.js' */` at the top and then use bare `Foo` in every JSDoc annotation. Do not write `{import('./types.js').Foo}` in `@param`, `@returns`, `@type`, `@typedef`, `@satisfies`, `@template`, or a bare JSDoc type. The [pre-push-gates] `no-inline-import-jsdoc` probe fails the gate on newly added instances; `inline-import-exempt` in a file's first five lines is the exceptional per-file escape hatch. Provenance: `endojs/endo-but-for-bots#75` review `r3223741240` and repeated miss `#721` (`packages/reminder/src/store.js`).\n- **Verify no open PR already implements the issue** before opening a worktree. This is the *semantic* duplicate check, distinct from `ensure-pr.sh`'s identity check (which knows only about PRs this job base opened): the cheap pre-flight is `gh pr list --repo <owner>/<repo> --state all --search \"<N> in:title\"` plus a search on the head-branch convention the project uses. Skip and surface the existing PR number if a duplicate would result.\n- **Pre-flight design-status drift.** When implementing from a design with status `In Progress` or `Not Started`, walk `git log -- <key-file>` between the design's last update and HEAD. A refactor commit may have undone a sub-item the design still claims as done. Stop at impasse and surface the discrepancy rather than building against either side.\n- **Check `Depends On` against the roadmap annotation.** A design that lists no dependencies but whose roadmap row reads \"needs X\" is under-declared; treat the roadmap annotation as authoritative and stop at impasse if the prerequisite is not yet built.\n- **Reconcile current related-design review state before declaring a slice independent.** A design document is a snapshot; a *related* open design PR's live review is the current maintainer direction. Before opening the draft PR, run the deterministic producer over the related design PRs this change composes beside (the siblings the seed design's `## Related` / roadmap grouping names, plus any the job body declares) — it is the horizontal build-preparation gate of [design-dependency-walk](../../skills/design-dependency-walk/SKILL.md) § Related-design review-state gate:\n\n  ```sh\n  scripts/jobs/gardening/related-design-state.sh <owner/repo> \\\n    --related <pr,pr,...> --worktree \"$PWD\" --base <base-branch>\n  ```\n\n  If it reports `related-design-verdict=attention` (exit 10), a related design PR carries an outstanding maintainer **changes-requested** review that may invalidate the seam you are about to build on. Do **not** assert independence from the design document or a prior report. Either (a) demonstrate *in the PR body* how this implementation still composes with every outstanding direction, or (b) stop and redirect behind the related design (surface the impasse in your completion report). This is the prevention for the `stale-related-design-direction` miss (`kriscendobot/minion.town#48`, where a build asserted its serving slice independent of the changes-requested design PR 47 and cleared four panel rounds before the maintainer closed it for reconstruction). Whenever the build proceeds against declared related design PRs, embed a `<!-- garden-related-design: <pr,pr,...> -->` marker in the draft PR body so the panel's review-cycle sensor rediscovers and re-checks the same set at review time.\n- **Modeled-on designs abbreviate their source.** When a design says \"this implements pattern X from package Y\", open Y's source files before writing the first line of implementation; sketches in design bodies routinely omit selector or fallback branches the source actually handles.\n- **A design that lives on the roadmap branch is read, not branched-from.** When a `build` job references a design that lives on a project's bot-fork roadmap branch (today `llm` on `endojs/endo-but-for-bots`), the implementation branches off the project's natural implementation base (`master`, or whatever the project's README names), not off the roadmap branch. The design and the implementation are separate PRs with different bases. Read the design file at its path on the roadmap branch (or in the design PR's diff); implement on master-base; do not combine the two PRs.\n- **Infer the base branch from package availability.** The \"implementations are based on master\" default applies when every touched package exists on `master`. When the implementation must touch a package that exists only on the roadmap branch (`git ls-tree origin/llm -- packages/<name>` returns a directory but `git ls-tree origin/master -- packages/<name>` does not), the base is the roadmap branch, not `master`. The per-project rule and its mixed-touch impasse case live on the project's journal-side README. Builders inspect package availability on both candidate bases *before* opening the PR.\n- Conventional-commit messages (`feat(<pkg>):`, `fix(<pkg>):`, `chore:`, etc.) with the issue number in parens.\n- Run the full pre-PR checklist before the first push and again before any body rewrite.\n- Verify regression evidence for every new test before pushing.\n- **Hand off to the panel when the draft PR is open.** The builder's last act in its stage is to surface the PR number and the affected packages for the gardener's next stages (assayer in concert if the jurisdiction calls for it, then the panel, then the fixer loop if the panel raises in-scope complaints, then cleaner). The builder does not run the panel; the gardener's gauntlet script does.\n- **Do not double back to fix the builder's own PR.** When the panel raises in-scope complaints, the fixer stage addresses them. The panel's whole point is independence.\n- **Type definitions belong in a `.d.ts` / `.ts` types module, not a typedef-only `.js` module or an inline `@typedef` in a `.js` file.** Per Endo house style, shared or exported type definitions live in the package's dedicated type-definition module (`src/types.ts`, which `tsc` emits as the `.d.ts`; or a hand-written `.d.ts` re-export index) and are pulled into `.js` files with a top-of-file `/** @import { Foo } from './types.js' */`. Two shapes are wrong, not one: (1) a **whole typedef-only `.js` module** — a `types.js` / `*-types.js` file whose entire content is `@typedef` / `@callback` blocks with no runtime exports (the `export {};`-marker masquerade). Author it as a hand-written `.d.ts` and repoint the package's `types` export condition at it (the [pre-push-gates] `typedefs-belong-in-dts` probe fails the gate on exactly this shape — it is deterministic and cannot be skipped). (2) a **multi-field `@typedef {{ ... }}` block inline in a `src/**/*.js` implementation file**. **Escape hatch:** a genuinely module-private, single-use `@typedef` referenced only within the one `.js` implementation file (never exported, never reused) may stay inline; the rule targets typedefs that are exported, shared, or describe a package's public data shapes. Mirrors the `@import`-over-inline-`import()` rule ([pre-push-gates] `no-inline-import-jsdoc`) and the makeExo-over-`Far` house-style pattern. Provenance: kriskowal first made the `types.d.ts` preference and `@import` repair explicit on `endojs/endo-but-for-bots#388` review `4413566645`, comments `3344377619` and `3344477615` (`packages/gateway/src/{bootstrap,uds-paths}.js`, 2026-06-02); repeated it on `#58` review `4612637233` (`packages/daemon/src/trace-aggregator.js:41`, 2026-07-02); and again on `#442` review `4629047816` (`packages/platform/src/fs/types.js`, the whole-module shape, 2026-07-02/03).\n- **Agoric exo interface guards match known static types.** When authoring an `agoric-sdk` exo `M.interface(...)` guard, follow [`packages/portfolio-contract/CONTRIBUTING.md` § `TypedPattern`s](https://github.com/Agoric/agoric-sdk/blob/master/packages/portfolio-contract/CONTRIBUTING.md#typedpatterns): the guard is runtime enforcement, so every argument and return matcher matches its known static type as tightly as possible. Reuse an already-exported precise shape (for example `FlowKeyShape`) rather than introducing a loose matcher; an async handler returns `M.promise()`. `M.any()`, a broad `M.record()`, or another loose matcher is allowed only for a deliberately designed compatibility boundary and must carry a code comment explaining why its known type cannot be guarded more precisely.\n- **Reach for an existing `@endo/*` utility before hand-rolling a primitive.** Endo ships small audited primitives — `@endo/bytes`, `@endo/hex`, `@endo/base64`, `@endo/ascii`, `@endo/sha256`, and the `Fail` / `assert` / `q` helpers of `@endo/errors` — and the house expectation is to depend on them rather than re-implement hex/base64/ascii encoding, byte handling, hashing, or error assertions (a hand-rolled `insist` is spelled `assert` via `@endo/errors`). This holds for Rust ports too: reuse the JS implementation through bundling rather than duplicating it. Before writing an encoder, decoder, hasher, or a hand-rolled throw/assert, check whether an `@endo/*` package already provides it and import it. Provenance: kriskowal across `endojs/endo-but-for-bots` #671 (`@endo/sha256`), #755 / #882 (`@endo/bytes`, `@endo/errors`), #824 / #836 (`@endo/hex`, `@endo/ascii`), #877 (`@endo/base64`). Sibling to the `named-imports-over-namespace` and `endo-errors-over-raw-throw` idioms.\n- **Use URL-relative math for module files.** In Endo JavaScript and TypeScript, derive files and directories from `import.meta.url` with `new URL(...)`, not `node:path`: `fileURLToPath(new URL('.', import.meta.url))` rather than `path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))`. Keep the value as a URL until a Node API needs a native path string. `node:path` remains appropriate for a path that already starts as a native string or an operation with no URL equivalent. See [url-path-math].\n- **Harden an exported exo / client capability structurally.** An exported client or exo object carries interface guards (`M.interface(...)`), hardens its API surface, and attenuates authority **structurally** — a read-only facet statically lacks write methods rather than gating them behind a runtime `if (readOnly)` flag (POLA: code that cannot write should not hold write authority; follow a mereology builder pattern `whole.part('A')`, cf `@agoric/pola-io`). A module reaching for ambient authority (`setTimeout`, unguarded globals) declares itself unconfined — justify that or remove it. Provenance: `dckc` across `endojs/endo-but-for-bots` #874 (no interface guards, unhardened surface) and #881 (`if (readOnly)` attenuation, `setTimeout` in a facet).\n- **Diagrams in READMEs and prose docs: use mermaid, not ASCII or line-art.** When the implementation lands a diagram in a README, package doc, or design-adjacent prose, reach for a `` ```mermaid `` fence. ASCII and line-art diagrams drift out of alignment as the doc evolves. Exceptions: inline directional arrows inside a sentence or code comment (`// foo -> bar`), pre-existing ASCII diagrams in files the PR does not otherwise touch, and tabular or terminal-log captures (those are data, not diagrams).\n\n## External-repo etiquette\n\nThe builder opens a PR on an upstream fork, which is implicit in the `build` job's framing. Posting comments, reviews, or cross-references on issues or other PRs requires explicit per-action authorization in the job body. See `roles/COMMON.md` § External-repo etiquette.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- The draft PR is open against the named base, with a title and body that follow [pr-formation].\n- Every new test is load-bearing per [regression-evidence].\n- A separate `chore: Update yarn.lock` commit when the change touched dependencies.\n- The report names the PR number and the affected packages.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
